key_equations:
RBTLL cumulative distribution function: \[F(x)= rac{e^{\gamma x^{
u}}}{1+e^{\gamma x^{
u}}}+ rac{p}{1+e^{\gamma x^{
u}}}\log\left( rac{1}{1+e^{\gamma x^{
u}}}ight)\] F is the cumulative distribution function, x is a positive observation, gamma and nu are positive distribution parameters, p is the transformation parameter between zero and one, e is the base of the natural logarithm, and log denotes the natural logarithm.

Reliability under shared gamma frailty: \[F_T(t|	heta)=\left[1+
u\sum_{j=1}^{J}\left( rac{t}{\eta_j}ight)^\gammaight]^{-1/
u}\] F_T is unit reliability at time t; theta is the model parameter vector; nu is the frailty variance; J is the number of competing causes; eta_j is the Weibull scale for cause j; and gamma is the common Weibull shape.

Fisher information for interval failure and survival observations: \[I(	heta|\zeta)=\sum_{i=1}^{M}\sum_{j=1}^{J} rac{E[N_i]}{q_{ij}}
abla_	heta q_{ij}
abla_	heta q_{ij}^{T}+\sum_{i=1}^{M} rac{E[N_i]}{1-q_i}
abla_	heta q_i
abla_	heta q_i^{T}\] I is the Fisher information matrix for parameters theta under design zeta; M is the number of inspection intervals; J is the number of causes; E[N_i] is the expected number at risk in interval i; q_ij is the interval failure probability for cause j; q_i is the interval failure probability for any cause; and each gradient is the parameter derivative of its probability, with superscript T denoting transpose.

Sample size for specified producer and consumer risks: \[n=\left[ rac{S_1z_ eta-S_0z_{1-\alpha}}{\pi_0-\pi_1}ight]^2\] n is the required sample size; S_0 and S_1 are the standard deviations of estimated reliability under the acceptable and rejectable hypotheses; z_beta and z_(1-alpha) are standard normal quantiles; alpha is producer’s risk; beta is consumer’s risk; and pi_0 and pi_1 are the acceptable and rejectable reliability limits.

C-optimal design criterion: \[\phi(\zeta)=
abla_	heta F_T(t_0|	heta)^T[I_1(	heta|\zeta)]^{-1}
abla_	heta F_T(t_0|	heta)\] Phi is the design criterion to be minimized; zeta is the test design; theta is the model parameter vector; F_T is reliability at the specified time t_0; the gradient is its parameter derivative; I_1 is the Fisher information per test unit; and the superscript T and exponent minus one denote transpose and matrix inverse.
